--TEST--
Core\Arr::min and max order by one total order where PHP's min and max compare loosely, so an empty, mixed-type or numeral-string subject answers differently
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Three separate divergences, all of them the same decision seen from different sides. **The empty subject**: PHP's `min([])` is a `ValueError`, and `Core\Arr::min([])` is `null` — ADR 0063 R5 makes `?T` the absence spelling, and "what is the smallest of nothing" is a question with an answer rather than a failure, which is the reading `Core\Arr::first`, `last` and `average` already take. **A subject whose values do not share one ordered type**: PHP's `<` converts, so `min([0, "a"])` is `0` (the `int` cast to `"0"`, then compared as a string) and `min([null, 1])` is `null`; `nvs_stdlib::ordering::compare_values` has one row per representation, nothing crosses between them except the two numeric ones, and a pair with no order throws — `rule:types/declaration`'s whole position is that a value never changes type by itself, so an ordering that decided `0 < "a"` by rewriting the `0` would be the one place it did. **A subject of numeral strings**: PHP 8 compares two numeric strings *numerically*, so `min(["1e2", "50"])` is `"50"` and `["01", "1"]` compare equal; Novis compares `string` against `string` bytewise and never numerically, which is the same divergence `nvs_core_arr_sort`'s doc comment owns for `sort` — the two members share one comparison by construction, so they could not part here without parting there. The agreeing half — every subject of one ordered type with no numeral among its strings — is asserted against PHP itself in `arr-min-and-max-match-php-min-and-max-over-one-ordered-type`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Show {
    public static function text(?string $found): string {
        if ($found == null) {
            return "none";
        }
        return $found as string;
    }

    public static function num(?int $found): string {
        if ($found == null) {
            return "none";
        }
        return $found as string;
    }
}

// The empty subject. PHP raises `ValueError: min(): Argument #1 ($value) must
// contain at least one element`; both members here answer absence, and `min`
// and `max` agree with each other about it.
array<int> $empty = [];
echo Show::num(Core\Arr::min($empty)), " ", Show::num(Core\Arr::max($empty)), "\n";

// Numeral strings, the sharpest form of the string divergence: PHP reads both
// operands as numbers, so `50 < 100` and its answers are the exact swap of
// these. Bytewise, `'1'` precedes `'5'` and nothing else is looked at.
array<string> $exponent = ["1e2", "50"];
echo Show::text(Core\Arr::min($exponent)), " ", Show::text(Core\Arr::max($exponent)), "\n";

// The same divergence over plain digits, where PHP answers `9` and `100` — the
// numerically smallest and largest — and Novis answers the bytewise ones.
array<string> $digits = ["10", "9", "100"];
echo Show::text(Core\Arr::min($digits)), " ", Show::text(Core\Arr::max($digits)), "\n";

// A leading zero, where PHP's numeric reading makes the two operands *equal* and
// its first-tie-wins rule therefore answers `"01"` to both questions. Bytewise
// they are two distinct strings, so `max` is the other one.
array<string> $leadingZero = ["01", "1"];
echo Show::text(Core\Arr::min($leadingZero)), " ", Show::text(Core\Arr::max($leadingZero)), "\n";

// An `int` and a non-numeric `string`. PHP casts the `int` and compares
// `"0"` against `"a"`, answering `0` and `"a"`; here the pair has no order and
// the member throws, naming both tags and itself.
array<int|string> $wordAndNumber = [0, "a"];
try {
    var $extreme = Core\Arr::min($wordAndNumber);
    echo "ordered\n";
} catch (Throwable $unordered) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// An `int` and a *numeric* string, which PHP does compare numerically — so its
// answer is `9`, an `int`, drawn out of a subject whose other entry is a
// string. The representations still do not match here, so it throws.
array<int|string> $numeralAndNumber = ["10", 9];
try {
    var $crossed = Core\Arr::max($numeralAndNumber);
    echo "ordered\n";
} catch (Throwable $notNumeric) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// `null` among the values. PHP orders `null` below every number and answers it;
// `null` is its own row here, so the pair has no order. This is the row that
// separates "the answer is absent" from "an entry was absent" — the empty
// subject above renders `none` and this one cannot render at all.
array<?int> $withNull = [null, 1];
try {
    var $lowest = Core\Arr::min($withNull);
    echo "ordered\n";
} catch (Throwable $nullPresent) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// The half that does not diverge, on the same members: one ordered type, no
// numeral among the strings. Both languages answer these, and they are asserted
// against PHP itself in the matching case named above.
array<string> $words = ["pear", "apple", "fig"];
array<int> $ints = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5];
echo Show::text(Core\Arr::min($words)), " ", Show::num(Core\Arr::max($ints)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
none none
1e2 50
10 9
01 1
refused
refused
refused
apple 5
